## Fun Fact: "Foreign Object" contamination is the third most common recall reason this year
With 98 reported cases, items such as plastic or metal in food are unsettlingly common. Among the latest, we see Straus Family Creamery recalling ice cream for metal pieces--proof that even high-end brands aren't immune.

## What's Cooking? Apparently, Salmonella
Class I recalls this year are at 173, with 6 new cases reported in the last week alone. Notably, JCB Flavors, LLC has issued recalls for multiple seasoning products, all potentially contaminated with Salmonella. A repeat offender? Perhaps it's time to review their processes.

## Mislabeling Mayhem
J and J Bunting, Inc. mislabeled sesame seeds as curry powder, and Market of Choice Inc. failed to declare sesame as part of their Za'atar spice mix. Allergens are no small matter, yet 256 allergen-related recalls this year suggest otherwise.

## A Tip of the Hat (or a Wag of the Finger)
Moonlight Packing Corporation takes the lead in our "Worst Brands" list with a recall score of 96 and 11 Class I incidents. For a detailed list, refer to our breakdown in the appendix.

## Monthly Snapshot
Recalls surged in May with 173 cases, likely due to a spike in allergen mislabeling incidents. Numbers have tapered off in June, but the month isn't over yet. Stay tuned.

## Final Thoughts
As always, the numbers tell a stark story. Food recalls are not just statistics--they're a reminder of the vulnerabilities in our supply chain. Check your pantry, stay informed, and don't let unsafe products slip through the cracks.
